\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE:  \highlight22\uc2 Argentina \highlight\uc2  Bonds, Peso Up As Global Mood Improves  \b0\par\par\uc2 387 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 September 2005\par 22:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Updates with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  results from Argentine \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 )  \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--Locally traded Argentine bonds extended their recent gains Tuesday as improved international sentiment continued to favor emerging market debt.  \par \par \uc2 The gains accompanied a pickup in demand for the Argentine peso, where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily to prevent a stronger rally. The peso closed at ARS2.9075 to the dollar, slightly stronger than its close of ARS2.91 on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Argentine bonds have been on the rebound since last week, when a decline in U.S. Treasury yields sparked investor demand for emerging market debt and fixed income assets. And, as U.S. equities rallied on return from the Labor Day long weekend that was aided by a decline in oil prices, the upbeat investor sentiment continued.  \par \par \uc2 The buying saw the most liquid issue, the peso-denominated discount bond, rise 1.14% to ARS124 by late afternoon, while the peso-denominated par bond had gained 0.84% to ARS48.2, adding to gains it saw on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 The par bonds are currently being bought ahead of its first interest payment since Argentina's restructuring, which occurs at the end of September.  \par \par \uc2 The same will be paid on the dollar-denominated par bond, which trades in the U.S. and which also gained Tuesday. It was up 2.02% at a dollar equivalent ARS37.8 by late afternoon.  \par \par \uc2 The same interest was not seen for other dollar-denominated bonds that don't face such a payment, such as the Boden 2012, up 0.03% at a dollar equivalent ARS256.1, and the dollar-denominated discount bond, unchanged at ARS98.1.  \par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, volumes showed a strong recovery from the thin holiday trading levels of Monday, said Daniel Bou Kahir of the Banco de la Pampa, who noted total turnover in the electronic market of around $180 million. Most of this appeared to be dedicated toward peso buying, he said, which likely drew the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in as an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar buyer.  \par \par \uc2 Later, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reported that it bought a total of $100.5 billion in open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , marking a larger-than-usual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 -By Michael Casey, Dow Jones Newswires; michael.j.casey@dowjones.com; 54-11-4313 1918 [ 06-09-05 2057GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050906e196000yf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
