dded.  \par \par \uc2 Prior to the entrance of foreign investors with the conclusion of Argentina's long-awaited debt restructuring this year, the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market had settled into a routine in which cereal exporters repatriated overseas dollar earnings and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  steadily bought dollars to maintain a weaker peso that favored exports. The government aimed to hold the peso near a 3-to-1 mark against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 But meeting that goal has become increasingly difficult to achieve as foreign investors flood in seeking peso-denominated bonds and other investments. Consequently, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has had to step up its dollar buying in recent months, only to see the peso reach its strongest point in more than a year.  \par \par \uc2 (MORE TO FOLLOW) Dow Jones Newswires  \par \par \uc2 07-21-05 1551ET  \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020050722e17l001dw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
