\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-IMF urges Argentina to tighten policies  \b0\par\par\uc2 477 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Juli 2005\par 00:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates with more details)  \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON, June 30 (Reuters) - The International Monetary Fund on Thursday urged Argentina to tighten fiscal and monetary policies to contain inflation, and repeated calls for a strategy to deal with creditors left hanging since a 2002 debt default.  \par \par \uc2 "Macroeconomic policy stance could weaken in 2005 relative to 2004, at a time when the output gap is closing and inflationary pressures are mounting," the fund said in an annual review of the Argentine economy.  \par \par \uc2 The IMF has warned that the balance of inflation risks had moved to the upside and could undermine growth.  \par \par \uc2 The fund urged Argentina's government to act on structural reforms to boost productivity and growth; to take steps to strengthen the financial system; and normalize relations with creditors to regain full access to international capital markets.  \par \par \uc2 Most IMF board directors said it was "appropriate" for Argentina to draw from its international reserves to service the country's debts, including repayments to the IMF.  \par \par \uc2 However, several directors urged caution saying such a move should not weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Argentina is one of the IMF's biggest debtors and owes the fund $14 billion of which $2.2 billion falls due this year.  \par \par \uc2 The government and the fund are preparing for talks in July on a new fund-supported economic program, possibly backed by an IMF loan, that analysts have said could help the country roll some of that debt over.  \par \par \uc2 The current program was suspended nearly a year ago as Buenos Aires hammered out a massive debt exchange with private creditors that was rejected by 24 percent of the bondholders.  \par \par \uc2 The IMF said the debt swap was an important step toward smoothing relations with creditors, but said Argentina should develop a strategy to deal with those still left holding defaulted bonds.  \par \par \uc2 It also urged Argentine to engage in talks with bilateral creditors through the Paris Club of western creditor nations.  \par \par \uc2 The global lender said the Argentine economy had bounced back, which offered the country an opportunity to leave the crisis behind.  \par \par \uc2 The IMF said the primary budget surplus of 3.8 percent of gross domestic product in 2005 meant a sizable injection of fiscal stimulus, but urged more.  \par \par \uc2 "(IMF) directors noted that further fiscal consolidation is needed to avoid budgetary financing gap from arising in the second half of 2005, and emphasized the need to maintain tight expenditure for the rest of the year," the fund said.  \par \par \uc2 The IMF welcomed the rise in interest rates but said additional tightening was needed as well as less \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has increased interest rates three times by a total of 125 basis points since January, but renewed \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  since April have accelerated base money.  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-\b \highlight22\uc2 ARGENTINA\b0 \highlight\uc2 -IMF (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|RNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050701e16u0001n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
