\par\par \b \uc2 Argentina's  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  policy: is it stoking inflation?  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Louise Egan  \par \uc2 781 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Juni 2005\par 17:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES, Argentina, June 30 (Reuters) - Argentina's two-year-long weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy has helped fuel economic growth through strong exports but now risks stoking inflation, analysts say.  \par \par \uc2 The International Monetary Fund has cautioned Argentina on its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy. The issue is expected to be part of upcoming loan negotiations scheduled to start next month.  \par \par \uc2 The government's target exchange rate of about 3 pesos to the dollar, aimed at sustaining double-digit annual export growth, is at odds with the regional trend where the major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have appreciated sharply.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has made record dollar purchases in the market this month to restrain the strengthening of the peso, boosted by a growing foreign trade surplus.  \par \par \uc2 To finance the purchases, the bank has to print more pesos, and that creates inflationary pressure, economists from Wall Street and the International Monetary Fund say.  \par \par \uc2 In the last five weeks the government has imposed new restrictions on incoming capital to curb peso strengthening.  \par \par \uc2 Yet the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  <ARSB=> has kept on gaining, nudging 13-month highs of 2.89/2.8925 on June 16 and up nearly 3 percent so far this year. This is despite other measures adopted, including new restrictions on incoming capital.  \par \par \uc2 "They're trying to block the sunlight with a finger," said Carola Sandy, analyst with Credit Suisse First Boston. "The market knows that sooner or later the peso is going to appreciate," she added.  \par \par \uc2 Exporters say the undervalued \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has allowed thousands of small and medium-sized manufacturers to gain access to foreign markets and boost economic development whereas many would have been hurt by a free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 But on Wall Street and at the International Monetary Fund economists say the government's excessive printing of more pesos to finance its dollar purchases is too quickly accelerating the money supply, injecting greater demand pressures in the economy.  \par \par \uc2 They suggest Argentina loosen its grip on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and thus ease inflationary pressure.  \par \par \uc2 In a report obtained on Wednesday, the IMF said the current policy "could be classified, under empirical rule, as a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg."  \par \par \uc2 "Staff stressed that continuing with such a policy risked that real appreciation pressures would materialize in the form of domestic price inflation," it said.  \par \par \uc2 Next month, the IMF and Argentina are to resume talks in broken nearly off a year ago on a new Fund loan agreement.  \par \par \uc2 The government has battled higher-than-expected inflation this year -- consumer prices have risen 5.2 percent in the first five months of this year -- through price agreements with key industries. The government sees 2005 inflation at between 8 percent and 11 percent; economists fear double-digit growth.  \par \par \uc2 Argentina "will be forced into a more active, anti-inflationary policy by early 2006." UBS said in a note.  \par \par \uc2 A NEW PEG?  \par \par \uc2 Argentina ended a decade-old \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg to the dollar, known as convertibility, in January 2002 in the midst of an economic collapse, triggering a 75 percent depreciation in the following months.  \par \par \uc2 But by early 2003 the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  succeeded in controlling the peso at about 3 per dollar through heavy dollar purchases in what critics call a "neo-convertibility" of 3-to-1.  \par \par \uc2 That \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  escalated this year amid a huge influx of dollars stemming from a large trade surplus -- $3.6 billion in the January-April period -- and first-quarter net portfolio inflows of $1 billion, according to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The bank offset those pressures by buying a record $1.58 billion in dollars so far in June. The peso has remained stable as a result and closed at 2.8875/2.89 per dollar on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 To mop up the excess pesos it is printing, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues more bills, or Lebacs, at higher interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 Some local economists and business leaders argue this "sterilization," or neutralization of new peso supply, is working. Inflation fears are overblown and the policy should continue, they say.  \par \par \uc2 "The key variable for sustaining the import substitution model is a weak peso and if you ask me, the model has been successful so far," said Maximiliano Ginzburg, president of MG \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  consultancy.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has eased in late June as the capital controls take effect.  \par \par \uc2 "My sensation is that the nominal exchange rate is going to stay at current levels. (Exporters) are not thinking about today's exchange rate, they're thinking about eight months, 10 months or two years from now and that's their conviction," said Marcelo Elizondo of Export.ar, an exporters' association.  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-ARGENTINA-PESO|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|U|D|M|RNP|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Do