\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Argentina \highlight\uc2  Bonds Dn On Crt Delay, Peso Flat In  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 582 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Mai 2005\par 22:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--Argentine bonds fell Tuesday as the apparent delay in a U.S. court decision fed into concerns about the fate of the country's debt restructuring.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Argentine peso was little changed, though traders said that was because of a fourth day of heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by authorities to stem the effect of massive inflows of dollars from exporters.  \par \par \uc2 Noelia Lucini, an analyst at Capital Markets Argentina, said the market had expected the U.S. Court of Appeals to have by now decided on a pending case concerning two bondholders' bid to embargo $7 billion in defaulted bonds tendered in Argentina's restructuring. A decision had been expected a few days after a final hearing was held on Wednesday, April 27.  \par \par \uc2 The delay is moderating the earlier optimistic expectation that the court's decision would come down in Argentina's favor and clear the way for the country to complete its restructuring, Lucini said.  \par \par \uc2 "Without any new developments in this case, it is natural that there would be something of a selloff," Lucini said, noting that investors are speculating that the appeals court judges are having a hard time coming to a shared opinion.  \par \par \uc2 She said most of Argentina's pre-restructuring performing bonds fell Monday. The dollar-denominated Boden 2012 was last quoted at a dollar-equivalent ARS246.50, down from ARS248.50, and the Boden 2013 was at ARS215.50, down from ARS217.00. The peso-denominated Bogar 2018 was at ARS125.30, down from ARS125.50.  \par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  - possibly helped by the government-owned commercial bank, Banco de la Nacion - again showed its capacity to offset the peso-appreciating impact of exporter dollars. The peso closed Tuesday at ARS2.8925, only slightly stronger than its close of ARS2.8950 on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 With a bumper harvest coinciding with tax season, during which exporters tend to repatriate their earnings to make their annual payments, dollar sales have been heavy over the past few days. On the other side of the flow, however, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been aggressively buying up those dollars, which has had the effect of keeping the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stable.  \par \par \uc2 Official data aren't yet available from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but traders are expecting to see similar dollar purchase numbers as those of the prior three trading days, each of which produced \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases of more than $85 million. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  makes such purchases with freshly minted pesos, thus using its foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to inject money into the financial system.  \par \par \uc2 "Quite clearly it is the policy of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the peso around ARS3.00 to the dollar," said Carlos Lizer, an analyst at local brokerage Puente Hermonos.  \par \par \uc2 Lizer said if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  starts to take a more sidelined role - perhaps to ensure that its new peso issuance doesn't exceed its own targets for monetary growth - Banco de la Nacion has plenty of cash with which to take over the role of weakening the peso. That's because the bank receives the deposits of the government tax agency, which are rising during tax season and which would simply enter a neutral money cycle as incoming tax revenue are used to pass pesos to exporters, who in turn use them to pay their own taxes.  \par \par \uc2 -By Michael Casey, Dow Jones Newswires; michael.j.casey@dowjones; 54-11-4313 1918 [ 10-05-05 2027GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050510e15a001h1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
