\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Argentina \highlight\uc2  Peso Gains Despite  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2 ; Bonds Mixed  \b0\par\par\uc2 471 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 April 2005\par 22:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--The Argentine peso gained slightly Friday, despite continued \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while locally traded bonds were mixed.  \par \par \uc2 The peso ended at ARS2.9000 against the dollar, slightly stronger than ARS2.9025 at the previous close. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has remained virtually unchanged since last Friday after it crossed the ARS2.90 mark that week on decreased \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and state-run Banco de la Nacion were both actively buying dollars on Friday, said portfolio manager Hernan Korin at Supervielle Asset Management.  \par \par \uc2 Traders say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buys dollars in the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to keep the peso from strengthening on heavy demand from exporters. Argentine President Nestor Kirchner has supported that belief by saying that he wants the peso to remain weak in order to make the country's exports more attractive on world markets.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has significantly scaled back its dollar-buying this year, however, to meet tighter monetary policy goals. Traders say that Banco de la Nacion, the Treasury's banker, has picked up the slack.  \par \par \uc2 Although Banco de la Nacion doesn't provide daily data about its dollar-buying, the Treasury reported Thursday that it bought $1.7 billion during the first quarter of this year - a significant increase from $129 million purchased in the same period of 2004.  \par \par \uc2 "They say there is independence, but it is a coordinated effort," between the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Banco de la Nacion, Korin said. "The state puts pesos into Banco de la Nacion and the bank uses them to buy dollars to keep the peso weaker than 2.9," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, investors in the local bond market remained largely on the sidelines Friday as they wait for a U.S. appeals judge to hear a lawsuit filed by holdout creditors who have delayed a settlement of the country's $103 billion debt swap.  \par \par \uc2 "It was too quiet today," said fixed-income analyst Alejandro Barros at Banco Piano.  \par \par \uc2 "I have the impression that there will be a positive resolution for Argentina. If that is the case, we should expect to see an upward trend for about a month," he said. "But we have to see what the judges say. There is a lot of uncertainty."  \par \par \uc2 A U.S. appeals court in New York is slated to hear the case on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 The Bocon Pro 12 saw the most action Friday, Barros said. The peso-denominated note was last quoted at ARS135.4, down slightly from ARS135.8 a day earlier. The Bocon Pre 8, another peso bond, was up at ARS161.5 from ARS161, while the dollar-denominated Boden 2012 was down at ARS243 from ARS244.32.  \par \par \uc2 -By Drew Benson, Dow Jones Newswires; 5411-4311-3127; andrew.benson@dowjones.com [ 22-04-05 2039GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050422e14m000vf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
