\par\par \b \uc2 Argentina stocks climb again, debt deadline nears.   \b0\par\par\uc2 369 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Februar 2005\par 22:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES, Argentina, Feb 22 (Reuters) - Argentine stock prices resumed their upward climb on Tuesday as optimism flourished with the country's debt swap offer four days before its close.   \par \par \uc2 The MerVal index of the 12 leading stocks rose 1.43 percent to 1,556.03, a hair off its all-time high close of 1,557.77 last Friday.   \par \par \uc2 The index has risen 13 percent so far in February as investors bet the government will get a strong acceptance rate for its swap of $102.6 billion of defaulted debt for $41.8 billion in new bonds, at a loss to the investor of up to 70 percent.   \par \par \uc2 "The market depends on the result of the debt swap," said Horacio Corneille, head of the brokerage of the same name. "If acceptance reaches 80 percent, the MerVal may decline because it already anticipated that. But if it is over 80 percent, we could have a new rise."   \par \par \uc2 Italy's regulators said Tuesday Argentina had reported that creditors holding $41 billion of debt were on board as of last Friday, equal to 50 percent of the principal or 40 percent of the principal plus past due interest.   \par \par \uc2 Analysts said many investors, especially foreign funds, will sign up in the last days.   \par \par \uc2 Volume on the MerVal continued high at 111.9 million pesos ($38.4 million) with buying concentrated in heavyweights.   \par \par \uc2 Petrobras Energia Participaciones closed 2.2 percent higher at 4.16 pesos, while Grupo Financiero Galicia, which accounts for 25 percent of the market, advanced 1.91 percent to 2.66 pesos.   \par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the peso eased as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and state-run Banco de la Nacion bought dollars to arrest the Argentine \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise, traders said.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed at 2.91/2.915 per dollar, 0.17 percent weaker than Monday's close. The peso gained 0.26 percent on Monday as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to the U.S. holiday.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  seeks to keep the peso weak for export purposes. But the peso has gained 2 percent so far in 2005 as dollars from grain exports and foreign investment funds flood the market.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050222e12m00n5h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
