\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Argentina \highlight\uc2  Peso Down On Ctrl Bank  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2 , Bonds Flat  \b0\par\par\uc2 635 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Februar 2005\par 20:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--The Argentine peso weakened in the final hour of trading Tuesday, driven lower by late dollar purchases made by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Argentine local debt was mostly flat as the fixed income market readied itself for the end of Argentina's $103 billion debt restructuring this Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The peso closed at ARS2.9225 to the dollar, weaker than its close of ARS2.91 on Monday. Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  entered the market late in the day to perform one of its customary dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is trying to maintain the peso ahead of the debt exchange," said Fernando Santiago Izzo of local brokerage ABC Mercado de Cambio. He noted that state-owned Banco de la Nacion also purchased the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 Many analysts expect the peso to come under upward pressure after the debt exchange closes on Friday. If the restructuring is successful, as most expect, there could be a large influx of funds into Argentine asset markets from foreign investors looking to profit from a post-exchange rally.  \par \par \uc2 Traders reported that volume recovered to average levels after a quiet session on Monday due to the U.S. holiday. Izzo said volume is likely to grow this week as exporters increase repatriations of overseas foreign earnings in advance of the expected peso rally. That, in turn, is expected to drive the peso higher.  \par \par \uc2 Traders say this could pose a significant challenge for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has long been the sole dominant provider of dollar demand in the local market, and seems to strive to keep the peso at about ARS2.90 against the dollar. To keep it there, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have to significantly increase its dollar purchases, which may put it at risk of surpassing money supply limits. [ 22-02-05 1939GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 The local market for post-default bonds was quiet, much as it has been for the past few days. Three-day settlement periods mean time has effectively run out for those looking to buy into the debt exchange by acquiring defaulted debt. This has effectively frozen the defaulted debt market, which in turn has sapped volumes from the performing debt market.  \par \par \uc2 But traders say that market is simply in a waiting pattern, with only days to go before the final exchange data are released.  \par \par \uc2 "Almost all the performing debt have been rising slowly, nothing great but in positive terrain, and mostly for the peso-denominated debt," said Javier Salvucci. "A positive result is almost a foregone conclusion now, all we need is the final level of acceptance, which should be above 75% and could easily be over 80%."  \par \par \uc2 He said the implied yield on the new bonds to be issued was above the current return on a lot of the performing debt, suggesting that the latter have been driven upward by fluid liquidity.  \par \par \uc2 He said longer-dated performing debt might "plateau" as investors seek out the new, long-dated restructured bonds once their secondary market opens after the exchange's settlement is completed in early April. However, shorter-dated performing Argentine bonds could continue to be driven higher if international conditions remain favorable, especially given that a successful restructuring is considered a boon to all classes of Argentine debt.  \par \par \uc2 Reports emerged earlier Tuesday that the government had told Italian regulator Consob in its weekly report on the status of its exchange that some $41 billion in bonds had been tendered as of Friday last week, the end of the fifth week of the six-week exchange. It was not possible to determine an accurate acceptance rate from that data.  \par \par \uc2 -By Michael Casey, Dow Jones Newswires; michael.j.casey@dowjones.com; 54-11-4313 1918 [ 22-02-05 2017GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050222e12m000wq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
