\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Argentina \highlight\uc2  Peso Loses Ground On  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2 ; Bonds Mixed  \b0\par\par\uc2 472 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Januar 2005\par 22:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--Another late-session dollar buy by state-run Banco de la Nacion pushed the peso weaker for the third straight day Thursday as some observers said that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's somewhat unpredictable \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this month has begun to discourage trading.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, bonds were mixed amid low volume, with so-called performing debt issued after Argentina's December 2001 debt default seeing some gains.  \par \par \uc2 The peso closed at ARS2.945 against the dollar Thursday, off from ARS2.9425 a day earlier, and amid continued low volume.  \par \par \uc2 Echoing an article in local newspaper Infobae, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader Carlos Diaz with local brokerage firm Giovinazzo noted that investor uncertainty, based on unpredictable \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has kept many out of the market.  \par \par \uc2 The uncertainty has helped push down trading volumes during this normally slow summer vacation month. Average daily volume this month is at $81 million, down from nearly $160 million in December.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has stayed out entirely for almost half of the trading days this month - a notable shift after it bought $1.4 billion in December - the largest month of dollar purchases for the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reported that it didn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Thursday's session, although Diaz said Banco de la Nacion did. The exact amount wasn't immediately available, however.  \par \par \uc2 Despite Thursday's decline, the peso has gained ground in the first weeks of 2005. This week, traders expect daily volume and gains to remain constant due to investor uncertainty about monetary policy and general waiting for news about the success or failure of the Argentina's ongoing debt swap. [ 20-01-05 2101GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 In the bond market, traders expect volume to remain low until the debt swap offering period closes on Feb. 25. As participating bondholders turn in their defaulted notes, liquidity in the local bond market is expected to drop off.  \par \par \uc2 So far, however, there is no indication of decreased liquidity, said Juan Ignacio Gomez Vega, an analyst with local InvertirOnline.  \par \par \uc2 "Many individual investors are being cautious and waiting for the deadline to approach. As it does we may see some contagion as investors identify indications" of a prevailing mood to participate or reject the offer, he said.  \par \par \uc2 In the meantime, performing notes saw some gains Thursday, while defaulted bonds were mostly flat with some losses, he said.  \par \par \uc2 Among notable movers, the benchmark dollar-denominated Boden 2012 was quoted in trading at ARS248.50, up from Wednesday's close at ARS246.6, while the Boden 2008, a peso note, was up to ARS110.25 from ARS109.88 a day earlier. Both of the Boden bonds are post-default notes.  \par \par \uc2 -By Drew Benson and Vanessa Nichols, Dow Jones Newswires; 5411-4311-3127; andrew.benson@dowjones.com [ 20-01-05 2131GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050120e11k000yo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
