\par\par \b \uc2 DJ  \highlight22\uc2 Argentina \highlight\uc2  Peso Loses Ground On  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2 ; Bonds Mixed  \b0\par\par\uc2 300 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Januar 2005\par 21:05\par \uc2 OsterDowJones Commodity Wire\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2005, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  Standard Attribution Statement: News Provided by COMTEX.  \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES, Jan 20, 2005 (ODC via COMTEX) --  \par \par \uc2 Another late-session dollar buy by state-run Banco de la Nacion pushed the peso weaker for the third straight day Thursday as some observers said that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's somewhat unpredictable \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this month has begun to discourage trading. Meanwhile, bonds were mixed amid low volume, with so-called performing debt issued after Argentina's December 2001 debt default seeing some gains. The peso closed at ARS2.945 against the dollar Thursday, off from ARS2.9425 a day earlier, and amid continued low volume. Echoing an article in local newspaper Infobae, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader Carlos Diaz with local brokerage firm Giovinazzo noted that investor uncertainty, based on unpredictable \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has kept many out of the market. The uncertainty has helped push down trading volumes during this normally slow summer vacation month. Average daily volume this month is at $81 million, down from nearly $160 million in December. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has stayed out entirely for almost half of the trading days this month - a notable shift after it bought $1.4 billion in December - the largest month of dollar purchases for the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reported that it didn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Thursday's session, although Diaz said Banco de la Nacion did. The exact amount wasn't immediately available, however. Despite Thursday's decline, the peso has gained ground in the first weeks of 2005. This week, traders expect daily volume and gains to remain constant due to investor uncertainty about monetary policy and general waiting for news about the success or failure of the Argentina's ongoing debt swap.  \par \par \uc2 (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 01-20-05 1601ET  \par \par \uc2 Copyright 2004 Dow Jones. All rights reserved.  \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020050121e11k000ep\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
