\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Argentina Peso Continues To Gain Against Dlr; Bonds Up  \b0\par\par\uc2 363 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 November 2004\par 21:04\par \uc2 OsterDowJones Commodity Wire\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2004, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  Standard Attribution Statement: News Provided by COMTEX.  \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES, Nov 17, 2004 (ODC via COMTEX) --  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to crank up its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Wednesday, but again failed to restrain the strengthening peso, which marked its fourth day of gains against the sliding dollar. Meanwhile, the debt market saw gains as investors sought out bonds amid tight supply. The peso closed at 2.9350 to the dollar, up more than a centavo from ARS2.9525 on Tuesday. Argentina's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchased $40 million on the open market Wednesday, the largest single-day buy since mid-June. The purchase marks a sizable increase from daily purchases of about $30 million in recent weeks. Under \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Martin Redrado, the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  has used large dollar purchases to maintain the peso in a tight range of about ARS2.96 to ARS2.98 against the greenback. As a result, the peso now appears cheap in comparison to the euro, and more importantly, the real from neighboring Brazil, as the dollar continues to weaken against these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Daniel Bou-Kahir, head of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  desk at local Banco de la Pampa, said that Wednesday's market was filled with peso-buyers, helping fuel the peso's drive upward against the dollar. He also noted that Argentine cereal "exporters are getting ahead on their liquidations," motivated by concerns about the dollar's continued slide. "The peso strengthening is more speculative than based on concrete news," Bou-Kahir said when asked about a Tuesday announcement by Argentine officials that Chinese companies are interested in investing nearly $20 billion in local projects. Instead, "exporters are worried about a weakening dollar and covering their positions," Bou-Kahir added. Along with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Argentina's grain exporters are the largest actors in the illiquid peso market as they repatriate dollar-denominated earnings. On Tuesday, grain exporters liquidated $63.9 million, a considerable increase from their dollar sales over the past week that hovered around $30 million. Wednesday figures were due in later in the day.  \par \par \uc2 (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 11-17-04 1454ET  \par \par \uc2 Copyright 2004 Dow Jones. All rights reserved.  \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020041117e0bh0066r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
