\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Argentina \highlight\uc2  Peso Stronger Despite  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2 ; Debt Mixed  \b0\par\par\uc2 502 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 November 2004\par 21:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--The Argentine peso strengthened against the dollar for the third straight day Tuesday as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  failed to offset exporter repatriations, despite engaging in some of its heaviest dollar buying in recent months.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Argentine bonds were mixed as investors awaited news out of a New York courtroom, where a judge Tuesday opened two lawsuits filed by disgruntled bondholders seeking an injunction against Argentina's long-awaited debt swap.  \par \par \uc2 The peso strengthened against the dollar to ARS2.9525 from ARS2.9550, even as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought $37 million - its largest purchase since Martin Redrado took over the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  in September.  \par \par \uc2 "There's been a recuperation of the peso and the market's volume has been due to a lot of action on part of the cereal exporters," said Daniel Bou-Kahir, head of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  desk at local Banco de la Pampa.  \par \par \uc2 On Monday, cereal exporters, who are the biggest dollar sellers in the peso market, liquidated $27.3 million in foreign earnings. Over the past week, they have repatriated between $27 million and $37 million.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , meanwhile, has been purchasing around $30 million a day in the open market in recent weeks.  \par \par \uc2 "The bank was there, but obviously not enough because the peso has strengthened," Bou-Kahir said.  \par \par \uc2 Expectations regarding possible announcements of Chinese investment during a visit by Chinese Premier Hu Jintao, who arrived in Buenos Aires on Tuesday, have had little effect on the peso market, Bou-Kahir said.  \par \par \uc2 Early rumors in the local media had predicted an incredible $20 billion investment package, but a visiting Chinese official in town last week rejected that as unreasonable.  \par \par \uc2 "Talk is different from action, and nobody is going to come in here and just fix everything," Bou-Kahir added.  \par \par \uc2 Hu and Argentine President Nestor Kirchner were expected to hold a news conference Tuesday evening to announce more modest investment accords. [ 16-11-04 2020GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 In the debt market, bonds saw a mix of minor gains and losses as investors awaited news about two lawsuits seeking to derail the restructuring of Argentina's $103 billion in defaulted sovereign debt.  \par \par \uc2 "Investors are waiting to see what happens in New York," said trader Paula Premrou of local brokerage Lopez Leon.  \par \par \uc2 Judge Thomas Griesa of the U.S. District Court in Manhattan was scheduled to open two lawsuits by disgruntled creditors on Tuesday seeking an injunction on the debt swap, which the Argentine government plans to open on Nov. 29 and close on Jan. 17.  \par \par \uc2 But Premrou said traders hadn't heard any news from New York as the bond market wrapped up for the day.  \par \par \uc2 In late-session trading, the dollar-denominated benchmark Boden 2012 was down 0.4 to 237.25, while the Pre-8, a peso note, was leading gainers, up 0.8 to 145.75.  \par \par \uc2 - By Suzelle Tempero and Drew Benson, Dow Jones Newswires; 5411-4311-3127; andrew.benson@dowjones.com [ 16-11-04 2028GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020041116e0bg000yo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
