\par\par \b \uc2 DJ  \highlight22\uc2 Argentina \highlight\uc2  Peso Stronger Despite  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2 ; Debt Mixed  \b0\par\par\uc2 346 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 November 2004\par 21:04\par \uc2 OsterDowJones Commodity Wire\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2004, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  Standard Attribution Statement: News Provided by COMTEX.  \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES, Nov 16, 2004 (ODC via COMTEX) --  \par \par \uc2 The Argentine peso strengthened against the dollar for the third straight day Tuesday as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  failed to offset exporter repatriations, despite engaging in some of its heaviest dollar buying in recent months. Meanwhile, Argentine bonds were mixed as investors awaited news out of a New York courtroom, where a judge Tuesday opened two lawsuits filed by disgruntled bondholders seeking an injunction against Argentina's long-awaited debt swap. The peso strengthened against the dollar to ARS2.9525 from ARS2.9550, even as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought $37 million - its largest purchase since Martin Redrado took over the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  in September. "There's been a recuperation of the peso and the market's volume has been due to a lot of action on part of the cereal exporters," said Daniel Bou-Kahir, head of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  desk at local Banco de la Pampa. On Monday, cereal exporters, who are the biggest dollar sellers in the peso market, liquidated $27.3 million in foreign earnings. Over the past week, they have repatriated between $27 million and $37 million. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , meanwhile, has been purchasing around $30 million a day in the open market in recent weeks. "The bank was there, but obviously not enough because the peso has strengthened," Bou-Kahir said. Expectations regarding possible announcements of Chinese investment during a visit by Chinese Premier Hu Jintao, who arrived in Buenos Aires on Tuesday, have had little effect on the peso market, Bou-Kahir said. Early rumors in the local media had predicted an incredible $20 billion investment package, but a visiting Chinese official in town last week rejected that as unreasonable. "Talk is different from action, and nobody is going to come in here and just fix everything," Bou-Kahir added. Hu and Argentine President Nestor Kirchner were expected to hold a news conference Tuesday evening to announce more modest investment accords.  \par \par \uc2 (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 11-16-04 1520ET  \par \par \uc2 Copyright 2004 Dow Jones. All rights reserved.  \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020041116e0bg0069j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
