\par\par \b \uc2 Argentina Peso Dips Again As  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Stays In Market  \b0\par\par\uc2 446 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Oktober 2003\par 19:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--Argentina's peso closed at its weakest level in over three weeks Thursday, as higher \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  activity once again outweighed the supply of dollars from exporters.  \par \par \uc2 The peso was one centavo weaker at ARS2.865 per dollar at 1750 GMT, shortly before the market closed. Tuesday the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  saw its sharpest fall in some weeks, dipping two centavos against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Wednesday the peso was flat.  \par \par \uc2 Traders say the key factor in this week's falls has been the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has stepped up its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  from some $5 million a day in early October to $35 million per session this week. With the supply of export dollars not lifting in tandem with the stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the peso has fallen back.  \par \par \uc2 That pattern continued Thursday, said one trader at a foreign bank in Buenos Aires.  \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  probably weighed in with around $35 million again. It seems to be keeping up the same levels that we have seen in the past few weeks," he said.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  nonetheless raise questions about the direction of the peso in the weeks ahead. Under last month's accord with the International Monetary Fund, the amount of pesos the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can issue to buy dollars is limited, with analysts saying Argentina's top bank has the space to buy around $15 million a day.  \par \par \uc2 That means the bank must reduce its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  from current levels if it is to comply with the accord, even if it means letting the peso strengthen.  \par \par \uc2 "These kind of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have to be sporadic and not permanent if \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  is to keep within the limits laid down," says Rafael Ber, senior analyst at local financial consultancy Argentine Research.  \par \par \uc2 And with exports likely to increase from November as the agricultural season gets into full swing, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may find it even tougher to stop the peso appreciating.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says it is not interested in any particular value for the peso against the dollar, but President Nestor Kirchner and other top officials have said they want the peso around the ARS2.90-ARS3.00. A weak peso helps exporters and companies producing goods that were imported before last year's 70% fall in the peso. Export duties are also a key source of tax income.  \par \par \uc2 -By Laurence Norman, Dow Jones Newswires; 5411-4311-3127; laurence.norman@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 As traders had predicted, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  later announced it had purchased net $35 million.  \par \par \uc2 -By Laurence Norman, Dow Jones Newswires; 5411-4311-3127; laurence.norman@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031030dzau0010c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
