\par\par \b \uc2  Argentine Peso Strengthens To ARS2.795/Dollar \b0\par\par\uc2 522 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juli 2003\par 20:50\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BUENOS AIRES -(Dow Jones)- Argentina's peso closed one centavo higher Thursday despite continued peso selling by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  At 1750 GMT, shortly before the local market closed, the peso was quoted at ARS2.795 to the dollar, up one centavo from Wednesday's close of ARS2.805 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  Traders said that market activity varied little from preceding sessions. \par \par \uc2  "We keep seeing the same pattern of exporters buying pesos and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  selling them," said one trader with a Buenos Aires-based brokerage house. \par \par \uc2  In recent months, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has adopted an \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy to soak up dollars being sold-off by exporters, who have to repatriate their foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Analysts say the monetary body's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  should ensure that there are no brusque movements in the peso in future sessions. \par \par \uc2  "This dollar-buying measure used as part of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  is going to keep the peso trading at these levels," said Vladimir Werning, chief economist for J.P. Morgan Chase in Buenos Aires. \par \par \uc2  And the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't suggested such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be stopped anytime soon. \par \par \uc2  Addressing the Argentine Senate on Thursday, \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Alfonso Prat Gay said the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  has set broad money growth targets for the year and expects to emit more pesos - ranging from ARS1.7 billion to ARS6.4 billion - in the second half of the year. Most of the money issued is used by the bank to purchase dollars. \par \par \uc2  Traders said they noted a pickup in activity ahead of Friday's Independence Day holiday in the U.S. \par \par \uc2  Volume running through the electronic trading system was $112 million, up about $12 million from Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  With the peso seeming to have found more stability in the last couple of weeks - after rising 20% in the first half of the year - there's less pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  as heavily as it did last month, analysts say. While the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought up $1 billion during June, this month has begun with the bank buying only some $36 million in dollars in the past two days. \par \par \uc2  Nonetheless, the peso's appreciation this year has provoked complaints from local producers who say it's hurting their competitiveness in international markets. And some analysts agree that the stronger peso is hurting an export-driven economic recovery from years of recession. \par \par \uc2  Yet, on Thursday, Prat Gay dismissed those claims. In his presentation to the senate, he reiterated that the peso's performance against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Argentina's major trading partners - Europe and Brazil - hasn't matched its appreciation against the dollar. Indeed, over the past year the peso has been nearly flat against the euro and has weakened in recent months against Brazil's real, he said. \par \par \uc2  "You shouldn't look at how much the dollar is worth in pesos, but how much the peso is worth in other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Prat Gay said. "One can't go about blaming the exchange rate," for the downturn. \par \par \uc2  -By Craig Fagan, Dow Jones Newswires; 5411-4311-3125; argentina@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030703dz730015u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
