\par\par \b \uc2  Argentina Peso Closes Flat Amid Modest Dollar Inflow \b0\par\par\uc2 289 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juni 2003\par 20:55\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BUENOS AIRES -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso closed flat Thursday, with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  pushing the peso lower in late trading after the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had edged higher at the session's start. \par \par \uc2  At 1750 GMT, shortly before the local market closed, the peso was trading at ARS2.795 to the dollar, unchanged from Wednesday's close. \par \par \uc2  Traders said a modest supply of dollars from exporters Thursday meant the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't have to issue too many pesos to hold back the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The only people that were selling dollars are those that had no other choice like exporters," said Adrian Rodriguez, a trader with a local exchange house in Buenos Aires. \par \par \uc2  Exporters have been putting upward pressure on the peso for months as they liquidate hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings back into pesos. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent $42.8 million Thursday to stem the peso's rise. \par \par \uc2  The Argentine \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been the principal buyer of dollars through most of this year, helping boost its reserves but placing more pesos into circulation. \par \par \uc2  Analysts have been eyeing these \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  because of concerns they could unduly accelerate the money supply and inflation. \par \par \uc2  On the heels of Argentina's financial crisis, inflation surged to 41% in 2002, and there were widespread forecasts that the country would relive its hyper-inflationary episodes from the late 1980s. However, inflation was brought under control from last September and the consumer price index has risen only some 2.5% in the past six months. \par \par \uc2  This improving panorama has given the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  more flexibility to act to stem the peso's rise. \par \par \uc2  -By Craig Fagan, Dow Jones Newswires; 5411-4311-3125; argentina@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030619dz6j0013c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
