\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Argentina \highlight\uc2  Peso Weakens On  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 313 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Mai 2003\par 20:48\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BUENOS AIRES -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso weakened two centavos Monday, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in the local market, \par \par \uc2  At the close of the local market, the peso was trading at ARS2.775, two centavos weaker than the 12-month high it reached at the local market's close on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "Today the tendency of the last few weeks continued," said Carlos Lizer, a trader with local stockbrokers Puente Hermanos. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the exporters are liquidating their dollars. There's no change in the market." \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought $126 million Friday and $1.2 billion in the month to date. While figures for Monday weren't immediately available, Lizer said he believes the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought a similar amount as last Friday in the latest session. \par \par \uc2  Exporters and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been the dominant participants in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market lately. Exporters have kept a steady stream of dollars coming into the country as they repatriate their foreign earnings, while a lack of payments on dollar-denominated debts means the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the only one looking to buy the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But a strong peso threatens exporters and import-substitution industries, two sectors that have benefitted from the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sharp depreciation against the dollar last year. \par \par \uc2  The International Monetary Fund is expected to allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to issue more pesos and expand the monetary base beyond previous targets. Analysts estimate the Washington-based agency will allow the issuance of between ARS3.0 billion and ARS4.0 billion worth of new pesos. The government and IMF officials, who have been meeting with Argentine authorities since early last week, may announce new official targets for monetary growth later Monday. \par \par \uc2  - By Wailin Wong, Dow Jones Newswires; 5411-4314-2272; argentina@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030512dz5c0016y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
