\par\par \b \uc2  Lavagna: Argentina Not In  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Talks With Brazil -Estado \b0\par\par\uc2 210 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Mai 2003\par 21:27\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO -(Dow Jones)- Argentine Economy Minister Roberto Lavagna Thursday said he isn't talking with Brazilian officials about adopting policies to control \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates, local news agency Agencia Estado reported. \par \par \uc2  Lavagna apparently discarded an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  plan presented Monday by \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Vice Foreign Minister Martin Redrado that would aim to keep Brazil's real and Argentina's peso at around three per dollar to facilitate trade flows. \par \par \uc2  Redrado's plan perplexed Brazilian officials, who have strongly stated they won't heed exporters' calls to forcibly weaken a real that has strengthened 20% since Jan. 1. \par \par \uc2  Lavagna also backed the idea of so-called four-plus-one trade talks between the U.S. and members of trade bloc Mercosur to complement negotiations for the hemisphere-wide Free Trade Agreement of the Americas. Mercosur includes Brazil, Argentina, Uruguay and Paraguay. \par \par \uc2  Lavagna held meetings Thursday with Finance Minister Antonio Palocci and \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Economic Policy Director Ilan Goldfajn and President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva. \par \par \uc2  Lavagna is visiting Brasilia with Nestor Kirchner, who is expected to defeat former President Carlos Menem in May 18 presidential elections in Argentina. \par \par \uc2  -By Terry Wade, Dow Jones Newswires; 5511-3145-1479; terry.wade@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030508dz580019s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
