\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Argentina \highlight\uc2  Peso Stronger Despite Near Record  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 720 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Mai 2003\par 20:41\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BUENOS AIRES -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso strengthened to a new 12-month high against the dollar Thursday, brushing off Tuesday's further easing of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls and some of the heaviest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on record. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have equaled the record single day purchase of $124 million set in January, as it tried to stem the peso's appreciation. \par \par \uc2  The peso closed the day at ARS2.765 per dollar, three centavos stronger than Wednesday's close of ARS2.795. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the peso is rising on investment from abroad and the closing out of long-dollar holdings. \par \par \uc2  It is not yet known how much the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchased on Thursday. What is known is that without today's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  activity of prior days, the peso would be considerably stronger. Many say that without \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it would be in the ARS2.50 to ARS2.60 range against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Since the start of the year, after it had become clear that last year's flood of money out of the country had abated, the peso has strengthened around 20% against the dollar. But even then, with an economic recovery helping stabilize local confidence, analysts tended to attribute the peso's climb more to a lack of demand for the dollar - because of constraints on companies' ability to pay their foreign debts - than from a strong demand for pesos. \par \par \uc2  All that was happening, they would say, was that the relentless repatriation of exporters' dollars - especially during the current harvest season - was providing a one-way flow that was not matched on the other side. Without capital outflows, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the only participant buying dollars. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, however, traders said the environment seemed to have changed, with several new factors encouraging Argentines and foreign investors to move into pesos. \par \par \uc2  One is that the dollar has been falling across the world in recent weeks. At the same time, the peso's rise - even during the uncertainty of a presidential campaign and after the easing of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls - has convinced Argentines who held onto the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the crisis of recent years, to trade them in for pesos. Traders also said that foreign investment in Argentina's rebounding financial assets is putting the dollar under pressure. \par \par \uc2  "We're seeing an unwinding in long dollar positions going on here and dollar inflows that are related to investment in local assets," said one trader at a foreign bank in Buenos Aires. "There is something new going on here." \par \par \uc2  The peso's rise Thursday follows the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to further lift \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls that were introduced in early 2002 to stem the sharp depreciation of the peso. The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lost 70% of its value against the dollar last year. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures gave exporters more time to repatriate their dollar earnings, made it easier to import goods and gave companies and individuals more freedom to buy dollars and pay dollar debts. \par \par \uc2  However, the measures did not manage to break the peso's rise, which some argue will harm the economic recovery by hurting the export sector and other industrial sectors. \par \par \uc2  An analyst at one Argentine bank said with neither the state nor companies paying foreign debt since Argentina's 2001 debt default, it was hard to see what authorities could do to stem the peso. \par \par \uc2  "For now, they can try and liberate more (controls) and allow companies to start paying their capital and interests payments. The truth is, no-one is paying anything here," he said. \par \par \uc2  The analyst said, however, that Argentine firms and individuals would only start considering paying their external debts once this month's second round of presidential elections was out the way and a new government was installed. That would mean the peso strengthening further in the coming weeks. \par \par \uc2  -By Laurence Norman, Dow Jones Newswires; laurence.norman@dowjones.com; 54-11-4311 3127 \par \par \uc2  Official figures released after the market close revealed that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought $144.1 million Thursday, surpassing the January record for dollar purchases by the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  -By Laurence Norman, Dow Jones Newswires; laurence.norman@dowjones.com; 54-11-4311 3127 \par \par \uc2  (Michael Casey and Wailin Wong contributed to this article.) \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030508dz580018e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
