\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Argentina \highlight\uc2  Peso Flat As Ctrl Bank Tones Down  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 405 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 April 2003\par 22:22\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BUENOS AIRES -(Dow Jones)- The Argentine peso ended local trading Friday flat on the day, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strong \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Thursday seemingly succeeding in signaling a ceiling for the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has strengthened 15% this year. \par \par \uc2  At the close of trading on the local Buenos Aires market, the peso was quoted at ARS2.905 to the dollar, unchanged from Thursday's close. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was far less active in the market Friday, buying just $4 million from banks in the open market and not buying any dollars from exporters. On Thursday, by contrast, in one of its biggest \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent months, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought a total of $22 million from both exporters and banks. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the message was clear that the government does not want to see the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthen much further. A stronger peso would undermine the interests of exporters, while some of the sharp gains have been at such a pace that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is believed to be concerned about monetary stability. \par \par \uc2  With Thursday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , "the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sent a message that they are concerned with the large gains in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said one trader with an international bank, who said he believes the monetary authorities will be content to let the peso sit at these levels for a while. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, analysts believe that uncertainty ahead of April 27 elections may start to do the government's job for it as investors begin to take more cautious positions. \par \par \uc2  For the time being, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is trying to get the market to function more independently. There's been a distinct lack of natural demand for dollars since the peso dropped 70% last year after a financial crisis in which the government defaulted on $141 billion in sovereign debt and abandoned the peso's one-to-one peg to the dollar; there's no offshore debt service payments being made by either the government or by many companies and there's very little importer demand for foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But because there's a big inflow of dollars for exporters, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has had to fill the gap. \par \par \uc2  However, traders said the flows were more two-way in Friday's market, with more genuine demand for dollars slowly surfacing. \par \par \uc2  -By Michael Casey, Dow Jones Newswires; 54-11-4313 1918; michael.j.casey@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030404dz440019v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
