\par\par \b \uc2  Argentina Seeks New Accord To Keep Fuel Prices Steady \b0\par\par\uc2 604 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 18:15\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BUENOS AIRES -(Dow Jones)- Argentina's government confirmed Tuesday it is holding talks with oil companies, aimed at extending accords that have kept the domestic fuel price steady in recent months, despite the huge volatility of the international oil price. \par \par \uc2  "We have spoken with producers and refineries," Cabinet Chief Alfredo Atanasof said at a daily press briefing. "You will note that during these months, the (international) price per barrel has increased considerably at some moments, yet the fuel price on the internal market has not gone up, thanks to the accords we've reached. Of course, we are working to extend these accords." \par \par \uc2  In late December, the government pushed an accord between oil refineries and producers, which sought to stabilize the domestic price per barrel at $28.50 for three months. Because producers fixed the price of crude, refineries were able to keep the fuel price steady. \par \par \uc2  The accord had several exit clauses, one of which was triggered if the international oil price went over $35 per barrel for 10 consecutive days. When that happened in late February, refineries and producers - under pressure from the government - agreed to extend the accord for another month, avoiding an increase in the fuel price, even while the international oil price soared to near $40 per barrel. \par \par \uc2  The international oil price per barrel closed Monday below $29 per barrel. \par \par \uc2  Atanasof gave no details as to whether the government was hoping refineries and producers would extend the exact same agreement as was signed last month, or whether an accord would have new elements. \par \par \uc2  President Eduardo Duhalde's government has insisted that oil companies shouldn't make extraordinary profits from the current international tensions surrounding Iraq. Officials have warned that rising fuel prices would spark inflation and seriously damage the many fuel dependent sectors of the economy. In addition, the government says, volatility in the domestic price makes it very hard for many businesses to make plans for the months ahead. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, for the second time in a week, Atanasof said he was unconcerned by the recent strengthening of the peso, saying it wouldn't bite into tax receipts and that there was no need for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise. \par \par \uc2  "No, we understand that it doesn't significantly affect the State's level of income." Atanasof said when asked about the effects of the peso's rise. "In any case, the value of the dollar is a value that is regulated by the market. As long as more dollars come in ... that price naturally lowers," he added. \par \par \uc2  Boosted by the inflow of dollars from exporters and weak demand for greenbacks, the peso has reached its strongest level in 11 months in recent days, closing at ARS3.00 per dollar on Monday. At 1650 GMT on Tuesday, the peso was trading at ARS2.94 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  Atanasof's comments mark a clear break with what senior officials have said in recent months. As the peso edged toward the ARS3.00 per dollar mark from the start of the year, the government warned that a stronger peso could hit Argentina's export-led economic recovery and weaken tax receipts. \par \par \uc2  Export duties account for some 15% of \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's overall tax income. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  strongly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market at the turn of the year to stem the peso's rise. In recent weeks, however, it has done little to prevent the strengthening of the peso, other than gradually scale back year-old \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls. \par \par \uc2  -By Laurence Norman, Dow Jones Newswires; 5411-4311-3127; laurence.norman@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030325dz3p0014q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
