\par\par \b \uc2  Argentina Ctrl Bk Says It's Beating IMF Targets - Reports \b0\par\par\uc2 845 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Februar 2003\par 16:56\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BUENOS AIRES -(Dow Jones)- The Argentine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president said the country isn't just matching but beating monetary targets laid down by the International Monetary Fund, according to media reports Thursday. \par \par \uc2  In a briefing to local reporters late Wednesday, Alfonso Prat Gay said he was slashing the organization's targets for monetary absorption in the first half of the year, as high tax collections meant the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was printing far fewer pesos than they had predicted would be necessary. \par \par \uc2  Last month, days after Argentina signed a short-term debt rollover accord with the IMF, Prat Gay announced that because of Treasury financing needs, which would require the printing of 3 million pesos ($1=ARS3.205), the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have to absorb some ARS4 billion in the first half of 2003 to meet the June IMF money-in-circulation target. \par \par \uc2  The money-in-circulation target was the key monetary goal the IMF set Argentine authorities as part of the IMF accord. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, however, Prat Gay said that after January's record tax collection, which left the federal government with a primary surplus of ARS829 million - the IMF primary surplus target was ARS420 million - he was revising his estimates. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  now reportedly estimates Treasury financing needs of ARS1.2 billion for the first half of this year, which means that the amount of pesos the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have to absorb will be just ARS2.2 billion by June. \par \par \uc2  With the new money absorption target in place, there will be less pressure for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise interest rates on its short-term government notes, or LEBACs. Demand for the notes has been relatively weak over the past two weeks, with potential investors staying out the market in the expectation that authorities will hike the rate as time runs out for them to absorb pesos. \par \par \uc2  "There is a perception that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is desperate to sell LEBAC in March and it is going to pay whatever rate. We don't feel that is the case," local daily Infobae quoted Prat Gay as saying. \par \par \uc2  The papers also reported that Prat Gay said Argentina's monetary reserves - $9.44 billion as of Monday - are around $1 billion higher than the IMF's minimum target for March, giving the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  the chance to sell dollars to absorb up to ARS3 billion, if necessary. \par \par \uc2  In a bid to build confidence in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Prat Gay said his organization would post on its Web site the new targets and the daily progress toward them. \par \par \uc2  With Argentina coming out ahead of its monetary IMF targets, Prat Gay repeated his prediction that inflation this year will be within the government's 22% target, rather than the IMF forecast of 35%. \par \par \uc2  The revised monetary targets were confirmed by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  later Thursday in a report sent to Dow Jones Newswires. \par \par \uc2  The revisions come days ahead of the arrival of the first IMF mission charged with reviewing the progress of President Eduardo Duhalde's government toward meeting the legislative, fiscal and monetary goals laid down in the IMF accord. \par \par \uc2  A team, headed by the IMF's Deputy Director of the Western Hemisphere Department, John Dodsworth, will arrive in Buenos Aires over the weekend, IMF Latin American spokesman Francisco Baker confirmed Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, Prat Gay signaled the bank will seek to end its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market and move toward a system of "free float." \par \par \uc2  "The price of the dollar must be fixed by the market," business daily Ambito Financiero quoted him as saying. \par \par \uc2  By the end of 2002, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was buying up to $120 million a day, in a bid to prevent the appreciation of the peso, which is considered to pose a risk to the country's export sector. \par \par \uc2  Since then, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lifted a range of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls in a bid to stimulate the market's demand for dollars. The authority's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market has since dropped to around $15 million daily in the last few weeks. \par \par \uc2  Analysts have said they expect the remaining \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls to be lifted in coming months. If that sparks stronger demand for the greenback, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could further reduce their \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  without worrying that the dollar would weaken. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso lost 75% of its value in the first half of last year, after the government ended an 11-year period when the peso was pegged to the dollar. In a bid to support the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , authorities introduced a range of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls, including demanding that exporters sell all their greenbacks to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  However, since last June, the peso has gradually climbed some 20%, with much of that gain in the last three months. \par \par \uc2  That has led to fears among industrialists that the country's exporters may lose their competitive edge and Argentines might start buying imports again, instead of locally-made goods. \par \par \uc2  -By Laurence Norman, Dow Jones Newswires; 5411-4311-3127; laurence.norman@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030220dz2k000zi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
