\par\par \b \uc2  Argentine GDP 4Q Grew 2% On 3Q - Lavagna \b0\par\par\uc2 1,085 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 2003\par 22:37\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BUENOS AIRES -(Dow Jones)- Argentine Economy Minister Roberto Lavagna insisted Wednesday his government would only meet upcoming debt payments when a debt rollover accord had been struck with the International Monetary Fund. \par \par \uc2  In a press conference at the Economy Ministry, Lavagna said press reports suggesting Argentina would use its reserves to pay some $2 billion to the Inter-American Development Bank, the World Bank and the IMF this week, even if a debt accord was not sealed first, were false. \par \par \uc2  "The Argentine government has explained its position various times ... It is impossible to make the payments outside the framework of a refinancing program" from the IMF, Lavagna said. \par \par \uc2  Argentina is seeking to persuade the IMF to roll over around $5 billion in debt payments to the Washington-based agency over the next six months. \par \par \uc2  Lavagna's comments come hours after the IDB confirmed that Argentina had not made a payment of some $681 million due Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The IDB press release quoted Lavagna saying he expected only a "brief" delay before making the payment. \par \par \uc2  Argentina argues that to use \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to make payments to multilateral without first having a debt rollover accord in place would leave the government unable to control monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  In the press conference, Lavagna said he did not feel hurried to seal an IMF accord by Friday, when Argentina must make a payment of around $1 billion to the IMF and a further $700 million to the World Bank. \par \par \uc2  "We'll keep working. There's no great urgency, neither for Argentina, nor for the IMF, as far as I understand," he said without explicitly ruling out whether Argentina would make either of Friday's payments. \par \par \uc2  The Economy Minister even said Argentina could make its payments before a deal is formally approved by the IMF board, as long as the IMF had already promised an accord. \par \par \uc2  Lavagna admitted policy differences remained between Argentina and the IMF, with the main disagreement focusing on monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  "There's a question about monetary expansion...whether to maintain more or less pesos in circulation," he said, detailing that the Washington-based agency was especially concerned about what Argentine authorities would do in the coming months. \par \par \uc2  The numbers of pesos in circulation has increased sharply in the past couple of months, as Argentina's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases dollars from exporters and consumers demand pesos to spend on their Southern Hemisphere summer vacation. \par \par \uc2  There are some concerns that the increased money supply could feed inflation, if demand for pesos drops off. \par \par \uc2  Lavagna gave no details of what the IMF had demanded in a draft of a final accord approved by Argentina Monday. \par \par \uc2  He said the IMF's permanent staff now has the draft final accord, drawn up by Argentine officials and an IMF team in Buenos Aires in recent days. The government is awaiting the staff's reply, he said. \par \par \uc2  Argentina has been seeking to wrap up an IMF accord for much of the past year, but a deal has run into repeated dead-ends. \par \par \uc2  Last week, however the IMF's executive board said they "looked forward to the early realization" of an accord with Argentina, reviving hopes of a deal. \par \par \uc2  Argentina is seeking to avoid defaulting on its debts to international lenders and being completely cut off from the international financial community. \par \par \uc2  In December 2001, Argentina defaulted on most of its $141 billion public debt. \par \par \uc2  A relaxed Lavagna also tackled a range of other policy issues during an hour-long news conference, in which the official broke off several times to joke with reporters. \par \par \uc2  Lavagna said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may order a further easing of year-old \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls, after two batches of measures in the past three weeks, aimed at making imports easier and facilitating Argentines' access to dollars. \par \par \uc2  "It's clear there is an over offer (of pesos). So there is no point in maintaining restrictions which limit the demand" for dollars, he said. \par \par \uc2  A further relaxing of controls could stem a recent appreciation of the peso, which the government says threatens Argentina's growing levels of exports and hits at tax income, which is heavily dependent on export duties. \par \par \uc2  The peso has strengthened nine centavos against the dollar in the past two sessions to reach ARS3.245 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  Lavagna also confirmed that Argentina plans to choose next week which bank it would ask to act as an adviser on renegotiating its foreign debt. \par \par \uc2  Lavagna said six banks had presented offers Thursday, each of them of "high quality." This weekend, officials will decide which of the offers met the government's requirements and next week, he said, officials will pick one of the remaining banks for the job. \par \par \uc2  Lavagna reiterated the role of adviser would be to prepare strategy and options for the government and wouldn't include conducting the negotiations. \par \par \uc2  He also promised that the government would make fresh announcements in the days ahead aimed at reordering Argentina's internal debt. \par \par \uc2  Lavagna also spoke about the economy, announcing that Argentina's economy expanded for the third consecutive quarter in the past three months. \par \par \uc2  Fourth quarter gross domestic product was up 2% on the previous quarter, while between April and December the economy grew 3%, he said. \par \par \uc2  "I believe this leaves no doubt about what direction things are going in," he said. \par \par \uc2  Argentina has been in recession since mid-1998. In 2002, the economy is estimated to have contracted 11%-12%. \par \par \uc2  Lavagna also said that between April and November 2002, 861,000 jobs were created, of which 530,000 were created "via public \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's official unemployment rate dropped from 21.5% in May to 17.8% in October. \par \par \uc2  However some say the jobless rate should be higher, if government-created "jobs" on public works projects are not all taken into account. Critics say in many cases, only the wages, not the jobs actually exist. \par \par \uc2  Finally, Lavagna referred to Tuesday's talks between Duhalde and his officials and Brazilian President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva. \par \par \uc2  Calling the talks very significant, he stressed the commitment of the two sides to move toward a common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and confirmed the neighbors would create a "Monetary Institute" to help them achieve that. \par \par \uc2  He also said Argentina's recent entry into the Andean Development Corporation, or CAF, would give the country access to around $200 million in public or private sector credits from the regional lender. \par \par \uc2  -By Laurence Norman, Dow Jones Newswires; 5411-4311-3127; laurence.norman@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030115dz1f001ce\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
