\par\par \b \uc2  Advisers: Scrip Makes Stable Argentine Peso Impossible \b0\par\par\uc2 1,053 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juli 2002\par 20:25\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON -(Dow Jones)- A panel of independent advisors charged with helping Argentina develop a strategy for rebuilding its economy said Monday the government may eventually use inflation-targeting to guide monetary policy, but not until it can credibly control liquidity in the economy. \par \par \uc2  Further, the panel said a degree of autonomy for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is necessary for an inflation-targeting regime to work, according to their report released Monday. \par \par \uc2  "A monetary anchor is a necessary (though not sufficient) condition for the restoration over time of price stability," the panel said. \par \par \uc2  "In the short-term, the stability of the monetary anchor depends largely on how the Corralito and the Corralon are managed," the panel said, referring to the government's freeze on deposits in the banking system. "In the longer-term, as is discussed below, it depends also on a fiscal position that strictly limits the need for monetary financing of the government." \par \par \uc2  The International Monetary Fund and the government of Argentina called on the advisory group of four widely-respected monetary policy experts to provide an outside perspective on the slow-going work by the two sides to develop an economic program. The recommendations dispensed by the panel, more general policy guidelines than a specific plan, don't differ much from the advice offered in public by IMF officials. For example, the IMF has frequently said that Argentina needs some sort of monetary anchor, or economic target, to help policy-makers control inflation. \par \par \uc2  The IMF has also said that Argentina would need to find a way to slowly release banking deposits into the economy as it recovers to avoid the excess liquidity that would fuel inflation. The government has had few takers of its voluntary program to exchange deposits for long-term government bonds. Meanwhile, money leaks steadily out of the banking system as deposit-holders win court injunctions, known as amparos, for the immediate release of the funds. \par \par \uc2  "The continuation of amparos is simply not compatible with adequate monetary control," the panel said. "Addressing this problem requires not just a temporary stay on amparos, but a clear strategy for preventing their reemergence." \par \par \uc2  Without forwarding a specific approach, the experts said it would be possible for the government to begin releasing frozen assets, especially those of small holders, increase the ability of deposit holders to transfer the deposits that remain frozen, and ensure that release of the deposits is controlled. Because it is impossible to say how quickly Argentines would voluntarily return freed-up deposits to the banking system, the government must have a plan for adjusting the amount of deposits released and a way to reabsorb excess pesos in the economy, the panel said. \par \par \uc2  But sales of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  to absorb pesos, a method used heavily in the past, isn't a good option for the government, because restoring confidence in the economy requires rising \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, the panel said. The country's large trade surplus should make building reserves possible, the experts said. \par \par \uc2  In addition, \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  should refrain from using \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in financial markets to support the peso, the experts said. The government should instead view the peso's value on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as a sort of progress report on its economic policies and adjust them should the peso weaken too much. Also, the group stressed the need for the government to control issuance of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  substitutes commonly used by some state governments to pay bills. \par \par \uc2  "The Panel wishes to underline that the development of a financial system based on a stable peso is not compatible with the continued existence of alternative \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues," according to the report. "The faster these alternative \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are retired, the sooner the basis will be laid for a credible national \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  Although the group said it is opposed to dollarization  for Argentina, saying that adopting the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  wouldn't solve the deep-seated problems in the economy and would limit policy options, it also noted that the dollar will continue to be used as a parallel \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the foreseeable future. Also, the group warned that inflation stemming from peso depreciation has yet to run its course. \par \par \uc2  Argentina's government will have to control spending to make a inflation-targeting regime successful, the panel said. While the government has no current need to borrow from the banking system to finance a fiscal deficit, that is true only because it has suspended debt service. Meanwhile, Argentina's public debt, both nominally and relative to the size of its economy, is growing as a result of indexation. \par \par \uc2  "All this means a sustained effort to improve the fiscal position on a durable basis is a priority, if the instruments of monetary policy are not to be overburdened," the panel said. "Argentina will of course need to aim in the years ahead for a fiscal outcome that is substantially better than simply covering expenditures before debt service." \par \par \uc2  Finally, the group said that for inflation-targeting to work, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  must have enough autonomy to make decisions free of political pressure and fear of personal consequences. \par \par \uc2  "Obviously such a situation cannot be mandated by fiat or achieved overnight," the panel said. "But it should be the government's objective to develop structures that allow the (Argentine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) to become an institution that has earned a level of respect now enjoyed by its peers in a number of other countries." \par \par \uc2  Respected former \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Mario Blejer has complained over the deterioration in \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  independence amid the deepening economic crisis. In particular, Blejer wanted \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials to have legal immunity for monetary policy decisions. He stepped down from his post last month. \par \par \uc2  Commenting on the report in a statement, IMF Managing Director Horst Koehler said the Fund and Argentina would continue to discuss these issues and "move toward a Fund-supported program as soon as the authorities are in a position to ensure their implementation." \par \par \uc2  Andrew Crockett, general manager of the Bank for International Settlements; John Crow, former governor of the Bank of Canada; Luis Angel Rojo, former governor of the Bank of Spain; and Hans Tietmeyer, former President of the German Bundesbank, formed the advisory group. \par \par \uc2  -By Elizabeth Price, Dow Jones Newswires; 202-862-9295; Elizabeth.Price@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020020729dy7t009ex\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
