\par\par \b \uc2  Argentine stocks soar on bank rumors, peso firms. \b0\par\par\uc2 369 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juli 2002\par 23:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BUENOS AIRES, Argentina, July 8 (Reuters) - Argentine stocks soared on Monday on continued talk of a new government plan to save the financial system from collapse, while heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  sent the peso slightly higher. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's leading MerVal share index closed 5.06 percent higher at 402.12 points in heavy turnover of 37.4 million pesos. Overall, 25 shares advanced, 15 declined and 11 shares traded unchanged. \par \par \uc2  Continuing a trend from last week, banking shares led the charge as investors continued to bet the government would force a switch of bank deposits into public bonds. Analysts say the unpopular move analysts say is necessary with banks short on cash amid a chaotic four-year recession. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president Aldo Pignanelli on Monday ruled out an obligatory deposits conversion, echoing comments over the weekend from President Eduardo Duhalde, but traders said they believed the government would eventually be forced to face reality and make the switch. \par \par \uc2  "The deposit switch is a reality the government can not avoid," a stocks trader said. \par \par \uc2  Bank Bansud gained 10 percent to 0.924 pesos, while Banco Frances jumped 6.2 percent to 2.23 pesos. \par \par \uc2  The MerVal is up 25 percent since Jan. 2 and gained 9 percent last week on growing hopes that local companies might be able to survive Argentina's crisis despite a wave of corporate defaults and fears of hyperinflation. \par \par \uc2  Argentina's peso firmed 1.4 percent to 3.57/3.59 per dollar in large-scale transactions in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market compared with Friday's close of 3.62/3.64. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had sold an above-average $49 million in dollars into the electronic wholesale market to prop up the peso. \par \par \uc2  The peso is about 6.7 percent stronger in the past week. The market was cheered by news of progress in aid talks with the International Monetary Fund and the government's decision last week to bring elections forward by six months to March 2003. \par \par \uc2  But the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still about 70 percent weaker since January's traumatic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation, which sent investors scrambling to buy dollars to protect their savings. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020708dy78015to\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
