\par\par \b \uc2  Argentine IMF deal seen way off as mission ends. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Simon Gardner \par \uc2 682 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juni 2002\par 23:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BUENOS AIRES, Argentina, June 21 (Reuters) - The IMF wrapped up a visit to Argentina on Friday under a cloud, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief poised to quit and no sign of fully-fledged aid talks on the horizon. \par \par \uc2  The impending exit of \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  head Mario Blejer - an ex-IMF official who is at daggers drawn with the government over monetary policy - is another body blow to the floundering government, as fears of Argentine contagion and debt woes in neighboring Brazil rippled through Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The government played up the visit by the International Monetary Fund, which arrived last week to crunch numbers and lay the groundwork for possible talks to restart frozen aid. \par \par \uc2  But there were no statements from the IMF that typically follow progress in talks to announce a move to a next stage - which Argentina hopes will be a fully-fledged negotiating mission. It remained unclear whether the Washington-based lender was satisfied with Argentina's promises and policies. \par \par \uc2  "I think it (the IMF) is still going to find that Argentina is far away from being ready," said Freddy Thomsen, economist at ING Barings in Buenos Aires. \par \par \uc2  Caretaker President Eduardo Duhalde, drafted in after an elected government was toppled amid bloody riots in December, is forging ahead with a plan to offer bank depositors bonds in lieu of their savings. \par \par \uc2  Argentines are furious at the confiscation of their savings, which have been decimated in dollar terms since January's botched devaluation followed a default on part of the $140 billion debt pile. \par \par \uc2  "The subject of the bonds for savings plan is closed," a senior economy ministry source said on condition of anonymity. \par \par \uc2  But the IMF wanted the bonds forced on savers for fear they would withdraw their cash at the earliest opportunity, turn it into dollars and stuff it under the mattress. That would weaken the peso, which is already down 70 percent against the dollar since a decade-old \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg was scrapped in January. \par \par \uc2  The fund also wants clear signs that a government pressured by growing poverty and unemployment will stick to vows to end the runaway spending that landed Latin America's No. 3 economy in its worst ever financial crisis in the first place. \par \par \uc2  Duhalde desperately needs IMF aid to stabilize the economy and fend off social unrest and the hyperinflation that gripped Argentina in the late 1980s - which could force his exit. \par \par \uc2  Neighboring Uruguay has already been forced to float its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  because of Argentina's spiraling crisis. \par \par \uc2  Any IMF deal - which analysts say would likely be little more than a roll-over of the debt Argentina owes it in coming months - is vital because other potential lenders like the World Bank are citing it as their cue to offer cash. \par \par \uc2  Markets are forecasting that Argentina's peso has much further to fall. The IMF has publicly said it wants the peso anchored by strict monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  Sources close to talks with the IMF have said the fund was almost certain to insist that \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  stick to a flexible exchange rate, vow only minimal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, limit how many pesos the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can print, and stop provinces from printing bonds as scrip \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  head's resignation - which a government source told Reuters would be tendered next week, after weeks of speculation - is yet another complication. \par \par \uc2  "Blejer's departure definitely would mean the government would be losing one of its most credible figures to negotiate with the IMF," said Fernando Losada, senior Latin America economist ay ABN Amro in New York. \par \par \uc2  "On the other hand the person who was leading the negotiations with the IMF was Economy Minister Roberto Lavagna in the past few days. My impression is the talks between Buenos Aires and Washington are not easy, but I wouldn't say that there's no hope." \par \par \uc2  Argentine officials say Lavagna should travel to Washington next week to follow up the fund's advance mission. (With reporting by Jorge Otaola and Guido Nejamkis). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020621dy6l013za\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
