\par\par \b \uc2  FEATURE-Argentina frustrated by failure to stop peso fall. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Simon Gardner \par \uc2 1,037 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juni 2002\par 20:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BUENOS AIRES, Argentina, June 17 (Reuters) - Paper IOUs in lieu of savings, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , scrambling for export dollars - \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  is frantically scouring for ways to stem the peso's free-fall since it devalued the national \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in January. \par \par \uc2  Experts say the government's tactics to support the sliding peso, which has lost nearly three quarters of its value against the dollar since being unhitched from a decade of parity with the greenback, could easily fail as they are just generating more uncertainty in a chaotic and recession-hit economy. \par \par \uc2  "Every peso I get I convert into dollars - whatever the rate," said exasperated depositor and lawyer Analia, who, like thousands of Argentines, saw her bank savings frozen by the government in December to ward off financial collapse. \par \par \uc2  The peso is now worth just 28 U.S. cents after Argentina's worst-ever economic crisis culminated in a default on part of the $140 billion public debt pile and a ruinous devaluation. \par \par \uc2  Caretaker President Eduardo Duhalde, Argentina's fifth leader since mid-December, desperately needs to stabilize the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fend off dangers of a return to the nightmare hyperinflation that gripped Argentina in the late 1980s, a scenario that could force his exit. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say a scheme to end the freeze but at the same time avoid a stampede to the dollar by letting depositors opt either for bonds and certificates or wait for the money to be returned at some point in the future is a non-starter. \par \par \uc2  "Accept bonds from this government? No way. That's madness. What are the chances of ever seeing that money again?" said Analia. \par \par \uc2  Their savings ravaged by the peso's demise, and with dollars second nature after a decade of using them interchangeably with pesos at one-to-one, few Argentines will ever trust their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  again, polls show. \par \par \uc2  Like thousands of Argentines, Analia plans to turn her money into safe-haven dollars if banks return savings as the government plans to allow. \par \par \uc2  IMF ADVICE SHUNNED \par \par \uc2  The IMF wanted Argentina to make the savings bond plan mandatory, fearing the public will take their cash at the earliest opportunity, turn it into dollars and stuff it under the mattress, further weakening the peso. \par \par \uc2  The dollar obsession had led some economists, and former President Carlos Menem, to argue that Argentines are voting with their feet by lining up to buy dollars. Why not make the dollar the official \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rather than the peso, they ask? \par \par \uc2  Duhalde is against dollarization, but while he has no plans to re-peg the peso, he has left the door open to a new fixed rate as statistics show that half of the 36 million population has been forced into poverty, unable to buy food or clothing as the weak peso has sparked an inflationary spiral. \par \par \uc2  With markets predicting the peso will trade at up to 9.0 to the dollar a year from now in the speculative forward \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets from around 3.6 currently, the stock exchange has suggested trading peso futures contracts to help stabilize the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by giving markets a heads-up as to where its value is trending. \par \par \uc2  As Argentines rush to buy dollars in the absence of an International Monetary Fund aid deal, analysts see the peso sliding further as a four-year recession grinds on in Latin America's crippled No.3 economy. \par \par \uc2  Near-daily \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has gnawed Argentine reserves to danger levels that experts say are near the bare minimum needed to underpin the economy. Reserves have fallen over 30 percent since the end of 2001 to around $10 billion. \par \par \uc2  "The government is sticking to its idea of a floating exchange rate, and all it is doing is losing reserves on the one hand and watching the peso lose ground on the other," said Carlos Arbia, chief economist with Exante consultancy. \par \par \uc2  Arbia favors pegging the peso at 3.0 or 4.0 to the dollar to end the endless \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "We're calculating by July the peso could be between 5.80 and 6.00 to the dollar," he added. \par \par \uc2  MONETARY EXPANSION \par \par \uc2  Argentina's leaders have made matters worse by printing money - either in the form of pesos or paper bonds used by the provinces to make ends meet and pay the workers of a bloated state - pressuring inflation and hitting the exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  Analysts estimate the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has printed 7.0 billion pesos ($2 billion) since mid-February amid a cash crunch - about double its original target - on top of some 5 billion pesos of scrip printed by poor provincial governments. \par \par \uc2  The government is also pressuring grain exporters to cash in dollar revenues to help prop up the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But farmers are holding off selling much of their output for fears earnings, which are in pesos, will be depleted by inflation or get stuck in the bank. \par \par \uc2  As a result exporters are bringing fewer dollars into the economy to buy the grain in the first place, which leaves fewer to be cashed in to help boost the peso. \par \par \uc2  "Farmers are holding back (their grain) ... due to the uncertainty, which means grain shipments are being delayed, which means exporters are not bringing in more dollars," said agricultural analyst Daniel Miro of Novitas consultancy. \par \par \uc2  The IMF wants the peso anchored by strict monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  Sources close to talks between Argentina and the IMF say the fund is almost certain to insist that \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  stick to a flexible exchange rate, vow only minimal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, limit how many pesos the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can print and stop provinces from printing bonds as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  An IMF rescue package is vital for Argentina, because other lenders like the World Bank that could potentially front aid are taking their cue from the fund. \par \par \uc2  "The fundamental problem is (that) the quantity of pesos constantly being dumped on the market is pressuring the exchange rate," said economist Camilo Tiscornia of thinktank Orlando Ferreres in Buenos Aires. \par \par \uc2  "A deal with the fund will not calm things in itself.... I think (the peso) is going to rise," he added. (With reporting by Walter Bianchi and Athena Jones). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020617dy6h0124m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
