\par\par \b \uc2  Argentina Departs IMF Meetings With No Cash In Pockets \b0\par\par\uc2  By Mike Esterl \par \uc2 888 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 April 2002\par 02:21\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON -(Dow Jones)- Argentine officials left the spring meetings of the International Monetary Fund empty-handed Sunday as the Fund balked at a new round of emergency financing and South America's second-largest economy sank deeper into crisis. \par \par \uc2  Managing Director Horst Koehler said he had "a very good  meeting" with Argentine Economy Minister Jorge Remes Lenicov  Saturday, but that Remes now had to return to Buenos Aires and "work further" before multilateral aid starts flowing south again. \par \par \uc2  Koehler added that an IMF mission would probably only return to Argentina in mid-May, making the government's goal  of securing more than $9 billion in stopgap loans within the next month increasingly unlikely. \par \par \uc2  "We are in a very profound crisis," Remes acknowledged to a group of reporters Sunday. \par \par \uc2  Better guarantees that Argentina's provinces will adhere  to tight fiscal goals remains a major sticking point - as does the absence of some fundamental numbers. \par \par \uc2  "The IMF needs to have a consolidated account about the fiscal situation in Argentina. This is possible, this is needed," Koehler said in a press conference Saturday. \par \par \uc2  The IMF halted a $22 billion bailout package in December  after Argentina overshot its fiscal targets. The country subsequently defaulted on $141 billion in public sector debt, before devaluing its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in January. \par \par \uc2  The situation has deteriorated since then. The IMF is now forecasting that the Argentine economy, in recession since mid-1998, will contract another 10% to 15% this year. \par \par \uc2  Argentina's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced late Friday it was shuttering banks and the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market indefinitely, and President Eduardo Duhalde warned Saturday "that the financial system could collapse." \par \par \uc2  Remes told reporters on Sunday he didn't know when the banks, which are widely regarded as insolvent, would reopen again. \par \par \uc2  Government officials were working overtime over the weekend in Buenos Aires to draft legislation that would force depositors who win lawsuits against bank withdrawal restrictions to accept their savings in bonds instead of cash. \par \par \uc2  Anywhere from $50 million to $200 million a day left the  banking system in recent days, even though the government froze most bank accounts in early December to halt a run on deposits. About a third of the seepage was due to court rulings overturning the restrictions, according to estimates. \par \par \uc2  Remes told an IMF committee Saturday that it would take 45 days for Argentina to craft a plan to rehabilitate the financial sector. \par \par \uc2  In addition to restoring confidence in the banks, the IMF is pushing \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  to revise bankruptcy legislation, adopt a less-\b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy and put  guarantees in place to ensure that foreign and local creditors get treated equally. \par \par \uc2  Its main demand, though, is that the federal government secure ironclad agreements with each of the 23 provinces on spending cuts. \par \par \uc2  The provinces recently agreed in principle to slash last  year's deficits by 60% and scrap a revenue-sharing pact that had guaranteed them a fixed amount of monthly transfers, regardless of how much tax revenue the federal government collected. \par \par \uc2  Remes said the federal government couldn't impose its will on the governors, some of whom have sharply criticized the IMF's austerity demands in recent days, but that it was planning a new round of accelerated negotiations. \par \par \uc2  "I believe there's a clear recognition that times are urgent," he said Sunday, adding that an accord with the governors was possible "in a couple weeks." \par \par \uc2  Ann Krueger, the IMF's first deputy managing director, promised the Fund would move rapidly to negotiate a letter of intent after the preconditions are met. \par \par \uc2  "The mission getting back there in May can be achieved quite quickly once there is action on the agreement with the  governors," she said in a press conference Sunday. \par \par \uc2  But Argentina's political landscape remains uncertain at  best. Last week, Alfredo Avelin, the governor of San Juan, roundly rejected the austerity demands of the IMF, exclaiming in a local radio interview that the "Fund can go  to hell." \par \par \uc2  Argentines are also increasingly frustrated with government decrees that have prevented them from accessing their savings. Already in December, four of Duhalde's successors resigned in quick succession because of violent street protests against bank withdrawal limits. \par \par \uc2  Brazilian Finance Minister Pedro Malan Sunday urged the international financial community to step up its efforts in helping his neighbor - but didn't sugarcoat the problems Argentina faces. \par \par \uc2  "The situation in Argentina is extremely difficult," he acknowledged in a press conference.  "Its complexity suggests that there is no easy and rapid solution." \par \par \uc2  Argentina also recently asked the IMF to spearhead an effort that would allow for a standstill in corporate debt obligations, as scores of local companies fall behind in payments in the wake of January's peso devaluation. \par \par \uc2  That plan appears to be grounded, though. Remes said on Sunday there were no discussions on the subject during this weekend's meetings. \par \par \uc2  Since the government junked the peso's decade-old parity  against the dollar, the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has shed more than two-thirds of its value despite a web of capital controls designed to stanch outflows. \par \par \uc2  "We will continue working," said Remes on Sunday, as he prepared to head back to Buenos Aires. \par \par \uc2  -By Mike Esterl, Dow Jones Newswires; 202-289-4187; mike.esterl@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020020422dy4m000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
