\par\par \b \uc2  REPEAT: Argentina Leaves IMF Meetings Without Cash \b0\par\par\uc2  By Mike Esterl \par \uc2 887 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 April 2002\par 02:25\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON -(Dow Jones)- Argentine officials left the spring meetings of the International Monetary Fund empty-handed Sunday as the Fund balked at a new round of emergency financing and South America's second-largest economy sank deeper into crisis. \par \par \uc2  IMF Managing Director Horst Koehler said he had "a very good meeting" with Argentine Economy Minister Jorge Remes Lenicov  Saturday, but that Remes now had to return to Buenos Aires and "work further" before multilateral aid starts flowing south again. \par \par \uc2  Koehler added that an IMF mission would probably only return to Argentina in mid-May, making the government's goal  of securing more than $9 billion in stopgap loans within the next month increasingly unlikely. \par \par \uc2  "We are in a very profound crisis," Remes acknowledged to a group of reporters Sunday. \par \par \uc2  Better guarantees that Argentina's provinces will adhere  to tight fiscal goals remains a major sticking point - as does the absence of some fundamental numbers. \par \par \uc2  "The IMF needs to have a consolidated account about the fiscal situation in Argentina. This is possible, this is needed," Koehler said in a press conference Saturday. \par \par \uc2  The IMF halted a $22 billion bailout package in December  after Argentina overshot its fiscal targets. The country subsequently defaulted on $141 billion in public sector debt, before devaluing its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in January. \par \par \uc2  The situation has deteriorated since then. The IMF is now forecasting that the Argentine economy, in recession since mid-1998, will contract 