eral government couldn't impose its will on the governors, some of whom have sharply criticized the IMF's austerity demands in recent days, but that it was planning a new round of accelerated negotiations. \par \par \uc2  "I believe there's a clear recognition that times are urgent," he said Sunday, adding that an accord with the governors was possible "in a couple weeks." \par \par \uc2  Ann Krueger, the IMF's first deputy managing director, promised the Fund would move rapidly to negotiate a letter of intent after the preconditions are met. \par \par \uc2  "The mission getting back there in May can be achieved quite quickly once there is action on the agreement with the  governors," she said in a press conference Sunday. \par \par \uc2  But Argentina's political landscape remains uncertain at  best. Last week, Alfredo Avelin, the governor of San Juan, roundly rejected the austerity demands of the IMF, exclaiming in a local radio interview that the "Fund can go  to hell." \par \par \uc2  Argentines are also increasingly frustrated with government decrees that have prevented them from accessing their savings. Already in December, four of Duhalde's successors resigned in quick succession because of violent street protests against bank withdrawal limits. \par \par \uc2  Brazilian Finance Minister Pedro Malan Sunday urged the international financial community to step up its efforts in helping his neighbor - but didn't sugarcoat the problems Argentina faces. \par \par \uc2  "The situation in Argentina is extremely difficult," he acknowledged in a press conference.  "Its complexity suggests that there is no easy and rapid solution." \par \par \uc2  Argentina also recently asked the IMF to spearhead an effort that would allow for a standstill in corporate debt obligations, as scores of local companies fall behind in payments in the wake of January's peso devaluation. \par \par \uc2  That plan appears to be grounded, though. Remes said on Sunday there were no discussions o