\par\par \b \uc2  Argentina's Peso Flat; IMF-Govt Talks For Aid Continue \b0\par\par\uc2 215 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 April 2002\par 16:48\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BUENOS AIRES -(Dow Jones)- Argentina's peso remained in a holding pattern Thursday as Argentine economic and monetary managers and an International Monetary Fund mission hold talks on how to strengthen the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Commercial banks quoted the peso at a mid rate of ARS2.77 per dollar, virtually unchanged from Wednesday's close. \par \par \uc2  Since devaluation in January, the Argentine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been hard pressed to subdue dollar demand and yank rival, provincial \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  out of circulation. \par \par \uc2  The IMF's chief negotiator for Argentina, Anoop Singh, is in Buenos Aires this week. The official said Wednesday that the IMF is providing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  here advice on future monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, some traders said that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market was "little moved" by IMF statements late Wednesday that the Fund is committed to aiding the crisis-wracked country. \par \par \uc2  At the same time, the IMF has hinted \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  should refrain from \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and let the market set rates for the peso. Other traders said the peso was stable against the dollar with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  forcing larger companies, such as exporters and banks, to sell off a large portion of their dollars. \par \par \uc2  -By Tom Darin Liskey, Dow Jones Newswires; (5411) 4314-2757; tom.liskey@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020411dy4b0016z\