\par\par \b \uc2  Argentina Throws Weight In  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Mkt, But Economy Stuck \b0\par\par\uc2  By Mike Esterl \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 778 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2002\par 00:31\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Argentine authorities showed Friday they can brake the peso's slide. Now they've got to figure out a way to get the economy moving again. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  threw a bunch of wrenches in the nascent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market during the last 36 hours, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly on Thursday by selling more than $100 million in dollars and then ordering banks to limit their holdings of U.S. dollars to 5% of what they had at the end of November. \par \par \uc2  The moves worked, with the peso strengthening Friday to end at ARS2.323 to the dollar after sinking below ARS2.40 earlier in the week as crisis-weary Argentines sought the safety of the greenback. \par \par \uc2  With more than $13 billion in international reserves, a web of capital controls that has tied the flow of capital into knots, and a seemingly infinite capacity to come up with new decrees like the one on Thursday, Argentine officials appear to have sufficient tools on hand to keep the peso's decline in check for some time. \par \par \uc2  "They're nowhere near the threshold," says Greg Anderson, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with FleetBoston, estimating that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely has burned through less than $1 billion in reserves in recent weeks. \par \par \uc2  Trading volume in the peso, which was released from its dollar parity in January, remains relatively thin and is almost non-existent offshore. Foreign investors aren't in much of a position to speculate against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  because  they'd have to borrow pesos, and there aren't any pesos to be borrowed. \par \par \uc2  Few think the peso would be trading under ARS240/dollar if the government didn't have so many checks and balances in  place. In a sign of how far the Argentine \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could fall, nine-month, non-deliverable peso forwards in New York were being quoted at a spread of ARS4.30-ARS4.60 Friday. \par \par \uc2  "One needs to understand this is political," says Alberto Bernal, Argentina economist at IDEAglobal, citing the growing anger on the streets as Argentines watch their savings evaporate. \par \par \uc2  Popular unrest has already prompted four presidents to resign since late December. Interim President Eduardo Duhalde, whose popularity also isn't high, vowed Friday the government will "make the dollar fall." \par \par \uc2  Economists say preventing the peso from overshooting also keeps a lid on runaway inflation. \par \par \uc2  But while they can gum up the exchange rate, Argentine officials haven't had much luck pulling the country out of crisis conditions after placing stiff curbs on bank withdrawals and defaulting on its foreign debt in December. \par \par \uc2  In Argentina, it's been a case of "plug one leak and another one springs open," says Larry Krohn, Latin America economist with ING Barings. \par \par \uc2  He says he's got no idea where the peso will end 2002, but he is forecasting a 9% economic contraction in South America's second-largest economy, which has been stuck in recession since mid-1998. \par \par \uc2  Argentine authorities briefly stopped \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the  market when a mission from the International Monetary Fund arrived last week, leading to speculation that the IMF was advising against using up reserves to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But that theory has been jettisoned in recent days, as \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  heightened its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  even as meetings were being carried out with the IMF on a new emergency credit line. \par \par \uc2  FleetBoston's Anderson points out that the IMF isn't against temporary \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in order to ensure a modicum of stability. Both Brazil and Chile adopted \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategies in their  local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in recent years with full IMF backing. \par \par \uc2  But new funding from the IMF appears a long way off, as the Fund continues to insist on the government crafting a comprehensive economic plan first. \par \par \uc2  That means the Argentine government might have to focus on a raft of structural reforms, which is sure to include another round of unpopular austerity measures as the country  tries to return to solvency. \par \par \uc2  Few economists believe authorities have a plan in place that would allow them to reach 2002 budget targets. Worse, they don't see the government making any progress on 2003, either. \par \par \uc2  "Nobody thinks we're going to be close to any of the targets this year," says Bernal, referring to the government goals of keeping inflation at 15%, the fiscal deficit at 1% of gross domestic product and the economic contraction at 5%. \par \par \uc2  "The point is getting the guidelines in place for next year," he adds. \par \par \uc2  -By Mike Esterl, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4026; mike.esterl@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020315dy3f001bk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
