\par\par \b \uc2  Argentina Peso Opens Up;Ctrl Bk Orders Dumping Of Dollars \b0\par\par\uc2 344 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2002\par 15:57\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BUENOS AIRES -(Dow Jones)- Argentina's peso opened stronger Friday on new \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulations that are forcing many banks to sell off most of their dollar positions. \par \par \uc2  Commercial banks quoted the peso opening at a midrate of around ARS2.325 a dollar for interbank transactions. On Thursday, the peso closed at ARS2.39/dollar for such transactions. \par \par \uc2  A resolution issued late Thursday by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  ordered banks to limit their holdings in dollars to just 5% of what they held Nov. 30. The resolution also ordered banks to sell off their excess dollars by the close of business Friday. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's resolution is just one of many desperate measures to prop up the peso, which has plunged by as much as 60% since it was devalued and began floating in early January. \par \par \uc2  For the most part, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been carried out through dollar sales almost daily, traders said. In the days after Jan. 11, when the peso began to float freely, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would sell around $25 million to $50 million a session, they said. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at its highest level since the January devaluation. Traders estimated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold $135 million to $150 million to keep the peso from a further free-fall. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  still closed weaker Thursday, at ARS2.39/dollar against the previous day's close of ARS2.36/dollar. \par \par \uc2  Analysts and traders have criticized the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  as wasting \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's precious reserves. \par \par \uc2  Jose Nogueira, head of brokerage ABC Mercado de Cambios, speaking in a conference Thursday, criticized the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. "You can't regulate a market by going out and tossing around $1 million or $2 million...Selling dollars at ARS2.30 when the market price was ARS2.40 - who does that benefit?" Nogueira said. \par \par \uc2  -By Dawn Weiner, Dow Jones Newswires; 5411-4311-3127; argentina@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (Tim Loughran contributed to this report.) \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020315dy3f000vl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
