\par\par \b \uc2  Argentine Peso Forwards Market Paints Gloomy Outlook \b0\par\par\uc2  By Mike Esterl \par \uc2 792 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2002\par 20:01\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Argentina's beleaguered \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  staged a rally in the local spot market Wednesday as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly and prepared to issue short-term debt securities to sop up liquidity. \par \par \uc2  But Argentine peso forward contracts traded in the U.S. suggest any recovery will be short-lived, as market participants continue to bet on steep declines against the greenback. \par \par \uc2  "Some people find the spreads too rich, but generally people would like to sell the peso," said Clyde Wardle, a New York-based emerging markets \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HSBC Holdings. \par \par \uc2  Argentina's spot \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened to ARS2.28 to the dollar in Buenos Aires trading early Wednesday, after closing at ARS2.41 Tuesday. Local traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  again sold dollars to prop up the peso in the local market, after selling as much as $80 million Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  By comparison, U.S.-based market participants say one-month non-deliverable peso forwards are trading at around ARS2.65 in the offshore market, which is free of the capital controls imposed by the Argentine government in December to stem money flight. \par \par \uc2  Things are seen only getting worse for the peso, with three-month, six-month, nine-month and 12-month NDF rates being quoted, respectively, at around ARS3, ARS3.75, ARS4 and ARS4.6. \par \par \uc2  Offshore volume is extremely thin, with daily volume in the NDF market estimated at around $20 million, and a futures market featuring physical delivery of the Argentine \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has yet to take shape. \par \par \uc2  "There is so much news and rumors coming out of the local spot market that people don't really know what to do," said Anshu Goyal, a New Jersey-based \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader with Lehman Brothers. \par \par \uc2  That has created some wide spreads between bid and ask rates on NDFs, further crimping volume. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the bulk of activity is focused on one-month NDF contracts, and is mostly comprised of dealers adjusting positions. \par \par \uc2  But the trend is definitely stacked towards dumping the peso, which the government began to float in January after scrapping a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board that had pegged it at parity with the U.S. dollar for more than a decade. \par \par \uc2  The country defaulted on its foreign debt in late December and saw four presidents resign in quick succession as Argentines took to the streets to protest a four-year recession and a freezing of bank deposits. \par \par \uc2  To right itself, the government is lobbying the International Monetary Fund to lend it $20 billion to $25 billion in emergency funding. But foreign investors aren't holding their breath, even as the Fund continues to huddle with officials in Buenos Aires. \par \par \uc2  "We continue to believe that a deal with the IMF is still far away from materializing," Goldman Sachs wrote in its daily emerging markets report Wednesday, adding that IMF mission head Anoop Singh has maintained a "severe tone" since arriving in Argentina last week. \par \par \uc2  IMF spokesman Thomas Dawson suggested as much in London, cautioning that the consultations are "not likely to be an extremely rapid process." \par \par \uc2  U.S. analysts believe the IMF will also urge \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  to limit its direct \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in order to conserve scarce funds. \par \par \uc2  In what could also bring new pressure on the peso, President Eduardo Duhalde said this week that Argentina hopes to eliminate limits on bank account withdrawals within three months. The curbs were put in place in December in order to prevent a run on banks, and have dried up liquidity in the local economy. \par \par \uc2  "As liquidity comes back in the onshore market, that'll also weaken the peso," predicted HSBC's Wardle. \par \par \uc2  If the controls are kept in place, the Argentine economy will continue to contract sharply, which would be another reason to sell, analysts add. The government has been forecasting a 5% contraction in gross domestic product this year, but many in the private sector predict the contraction will be twice that. \par \par \uc2  In a bid to blunt peso-selling pressures in Buenos Aires and create an interest rate benchmark, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday planned to offer investors ARS50 million in seven-day bills and $25 million in dollar-denominated securities. \par \par \uc2  Lehman's Goyal said there "definitely" was no interest among U.S. investors to take on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  notes, given the continued uncertainty in Argentina. \par \par \uc2  Even in Argentina, participation will likely be limited to state-owned banks and other entities with no choice in the matter, Wardle added. \par \par \uc2  Wardle said HSBC expects the peso to end the year at between ARS3.50 and ARS4 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  -By Mike Esterl, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4026; mike.esterl@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020313dy3d001el\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
