\par\par \b \uc2  Argentine Peso Opens Flat; Duhalde Seeks  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 193 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2002\par 15:22\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BUENOS AIRES -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso opened little  changed Tuesday even after President Eduardo Duhalde said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should renew its efforts to halt its slide. \par \par \uc2  After about an hour of trading, commercial banks were selling dollars to their largest customers at about ARS2.35,  the same rate as Monday's close. Banks were buying dollars at about ARS2.30, a little higher than Monday's closing average price of about ARS2.27. \par \par \uc2  Earlier Tuesday, President Eduardo Duhalde told Radio Continental: "It's time for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 "  after the peso tumbled Monday to the same level against the  dollar as the Brazilian real. \par \par \uc2  So far, there was no evidence of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar sales, traders said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials have said that they will not confirm or deny reports the bank has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Late Monday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  notified banks and companies that it was lifting some controls on dollar purchases and their transfer overseas, developments which could fuel increased demand for the greenback. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Tim Loughran, Dow Jones Newswires; 5411-4313-1918;\par argentina@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020312dy3c000vw\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusa